          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What is called
            %em inhomogeneous cosmology
            is the study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            via cosmological solutions to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %em without
            assuming or constraining these solutions to be spatially homogeneous (in the technical sense).
          %p
            This is in contrast to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein%27s+equations"} Einstein's equations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %em is
            assumed to be spatially homogeneous (an assumption also known as the
            = succeed ")." do
              %em cosmological principle
          %p
            Of course the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            is clearly not
            %em exactly
            homogeneous (due to initial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CMB"} CMB
            fluctuation and ensuing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+formation"} structure formation
            – see for instance the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KBC+void"} KBC void
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KBC13"} KBC 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SHRMS22"} SHRMS22
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AluriEtAl22"} Aluri et al. 2022
            %a{:href => "#Peebles22"} Peebles 2022
            for extensive survey of observed inhomogeneity, anisotropy and other deviations) – but the question is whether on cosmic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scales"} scales
            the deviation from homogeneity is small enough that it may be neglected, to good approximation, for the purpose of modelling cosmological evolution, or whether it exerts relevant “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/backreaction"}> backreaction
            ” on the global evolution of spacetime. For a clean account of the question see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#KolbMarraMatarrese10"} Kolb-Marra-Matarrese 10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertRasanen11"} Buchert-Räsänen 11
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Ellis18"} Ellis 18
          %p
            It has been shown that the effect of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/backreaction"} backreaction
            is small or invisible if the inhomogeneity is modeled in a non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            (i.e. Newtonian) limit, instead of taking
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} relativity
            into account (
            %a{:href => "#Buchert00"}> Buchert 00
            ,
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertEhlers95"} Buchert-Ehlers 95
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            to data.
          %h2#EffectiveDarkEnergyFromInhomogeneity Effective dark energy from inhomogeneity?
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            assumes that cosmic inhomogeneities may be neglected to zeroth order, and studies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+formation"} structure formation
            as a perturbation about a spatially homogeneous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FRW+model"} FRW model
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            Given that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
            faces some issues (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "standard+model+of+cosmology#BCKRW15"} BCKRW 15
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#RiessEtAl16"} Riess et. al. 16
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#MRS21"} MRS 21
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
            (a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmic+inflation"} cosmic inflation
            and the observational preference for
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phantom+dark+energy"} phantom dark energy
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
          %p
            References suggesting/discussing/checking this idea include the following:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Celerier00"} Célérier 00
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Buchert00"} Buchert 00
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wetterich01"} Wetterich 01
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Schwarz02"} Schwarz 02
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen03"} Räsänen 03
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AlnesAmarzguiouiGron06"} Alnes-Amarzguioui-Gron 06
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AlnesAmarzguioui06"} Alnes-Amarzguioui 06
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertLarenaAlimi06"} Buchert-Larena-Alimi 06
            %a{:href => "#EnqvistMattsson06"} Enqvist-Mattsson 06
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Buchert07"} Buchert 07
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sarkar08"} Sarkar 08
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Notari09"} Notari 09
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Blomqvist10"} Blomqvist 10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Buchert11"} Buchert 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertRasanen11"} Buchert-Räsänen 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Scharf13"} Scharf 13
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SmollerTempleVogler14"} Smoller-Temple-Vogler 14
            %a{:href => "#Ludwick15"} Ludwick 15
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Moffat16"} Moffat 16
            %a{:href => "#Ludwick18"} Ludwick 18
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SarkarEtAl18"} Colin-Mohayaee-Rameez-Sarkar 19
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ColinMoymhayeeRamezSarkar19"} Colin-Mohayaee-Rameez-Sarkar 19b
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sarkar18"} Sarkar 18
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Lombriser19"} Lombriser 19
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Deledicque19"} Deledicque 19
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#HeinesenBuchert20"} Heinesen-Buchert 20
          %p
            Discussion specifically of cosmic inhomogenity as the possible cause of the
            %a{:href => "Hubble+constant#H0TensionReferences"} H0-tension
            is in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Bolejko17"} Bolejko 17
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SikoraGlod20"} Sikora-Glod 20
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#WhitePaper22"} SnowmassWhitePaper22, Section VII.H
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Koksbang19"} Koksbang 19, p. 3
          %blockquote
            %p
              Cosmic backreaction is particularly interesting because it in principle has the potential to explain the apparent accelerated expansion of the Universe without introducing any exotic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
              component as well as possibly being able to mimic
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            %p
              Less ambitiously, cosmic backreaction might solve the
              = succeed "-problem" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
              = succeed ")," do
                %a{:href => "#Bolejko17"} Bolejko 17
            %p Whichever is the case, a theoretical quantification of cosmic backreaction is necessary for getting the foundations of cosmology onto solid ground; the mathematics clearly shows that in principle backreaction terms affect the overall dynamics of the Universe. It is therefore an important goal of cosmologists to obtain a theoretical understanding of the size of cosmic backreaction in the real universe similarly to e.g. the desire to theoretically understand the value of the vacuum energy density.
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#CosmoBack18"} CosmoBack 18
          %blockquote
            %p Since the early 2000’s a large debate emerged in the cosmology community around the so-called «averaging problem» (Buchert 2000; Ellis &amp; Buchert 2005), a question introduced on a more general ground in general relativity by G.F.R. Ellis in 1962. Because on the non-linearity of the Einstein equations, a non-trivial backreaction effect of the small-scale matter inhomogeneities is expected on the average large-scale dynamics of the spacetime. The occurrence of the late-time accelerated expansion of the universe at the same epoch when structure formation becomes non-linear is a tempting coincidence encouraging the backreaction conjecture against the real need of a dark energy component, which is needed instead in a Friedmann-Lemaître-Robertson-Walker setting. Besides, the same backreaction mechanism also accounts for an energy source dynamically equivalent to the dark matter. These arguments are often supported by toy models but not demonstrated in general, both from a theoretical and observational point-of-view. Simplified inhomogeneous models have been proposed to interpret the non-trivial dynamics of structures and the light propagation on cosmological scales. Only recently cosmological fully general-relativistic N-body simulations have been realised, offering a valuable ground to investigate the geometry of a realistic, “lumpy” universe.
          %h3#qualitative_mechanism Qualitative mechanism
          %p#Qualitative
            A qualitative discussion of how cosmic inhomogeneity may cause accelerated cosmic expansion is given in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen10"} Räsänen 10, section 3: “Understanding acceleration”
          %blockquote
            %p
              In general, underdense regions
              = succeed "\u201Cvoids\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              are negatively curved and expand faster than the average, while overdense regions are positively curved and expand slower. (
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen03"}> Räsänen 03, p. 15
              )
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              as the volume occupied by
              = succeed "inhomogeneous" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              structures grows (along with the density contrast of typical structures), the expansion rate becomes dominated by voids, since their volume is large
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              overdense regions slow down more as their density contrast grows, and eventually they turn around and collapse to form stable structures. Underdense regions become ever emptier, and their deceleration decreases. Regions thus become more differentiated, and the variance of the expansion rate grows. (
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen03"}> Räsänen 03, p. 25
              )
            %p
              In an inhomogeneous space, different regions expand at different rates. Regions with faster expansion rate increase their volume more rapidly, by definition. Therefore the fraction of volume in faster expanding regions rises, so the average expansion rate can rise (
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen10"}> Räsänen 10, p. 8
              )
            %p The acceleration is not due to regions speeding up locally, but due to the slower region becoming less represented in the average. First the overdense region brings down the expansion rate, but its fraction of the volume falls because of the slower expansion, so eventually the underdense region takes over and the average expansion rate rises.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              After the overdense region stops being important, the expansion rate will be given by the underdense region alone, and the expansion will again decelerate. Acceleration is a transient phenomenon associated with the volume becoming dominated by the underdense region.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              Whether the expansion accelerates depends on how rapidly the faster expanding regions catch up with the slower ones, roughly speaking by how steeply the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H t
              curve rises. This is why the variance contributes positively to acceleration: the larger the variance, the bigger the difference between fast and slow regions, and the more rapidly the fast regions take over.
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              So there is no ambiguity: accelerated average expansion due to inhomogeneities is possible. The question is whether the distribution of structures in the universe is such that this mechanism is realised (
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen10"}> Räsänen 10, p. 10
              )
          %h3#data_and_models Data and models
          %p#AbstractSmollerTempleVogler14
            An analytic proof of this qualitative picture is claimed in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#SmollerTempleVogler14"} Smoller-Temple-Vogler 14
          %blockquote
            %p
              Our analysis is based on the discovery of a closed ansatz for perturbations of the SM during the p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} = 0
              epoch of the Big Bang which triggers instabilities that create unexpectedly large regions of accelerated uniform expansion within Einstein’s original theory without the cosmological constant. We prove that these accelerated regions introduce precisely the same range of corrections to redshift vs luminosity as are produced by the cosmological constant in the theory of Dark Energy.
          %p
            A survey of the field of inhomogeneous cosmology and of attitudes in the community towards open issues, as of 2016, is in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BelejkoKorzynski16"} Belejko-Korzyński 16
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#SikoraGlod20"} Sikora-Glod 20, Section 1
          %p
            A similar conclusion is reached in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SarkarEtAl18"} Colin-Mohayaee-Rameez-Sarkar 19
            %a{:href => "#Sarkar18"} Sarkar 18, slide 44
            is summarized as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p#SarkarFlowQuote
              There is a dipole in the recession velocities of host galaxies of supernovae
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⇒
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Rightarrow
              we are in a “bulk flow” stretching out
              %em well
              beyond the expected scale (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                    %mn 100
                    %mi Mpc
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim 100 Mpc
              ) at which the universe is expected to become statistically homogeneous. The inference that the Hubble expansion rate is accelerating may be an artefact of the local bulk flow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              The “standard” assumptions of exact isotropy and homogeneity are
              %em
                questionable
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
          %p
            and a potential fallacy in the widely accepted interpretation of the data is claimed, which in
            %a{:href => "#Rameez19"} Rameez 19
            is summarized as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a{:href => "#EllisStoeger87"} The Fitting Problem in cosmology
              was written in 1987. In the context of this work and the significant theoretical difficulties involved in inferring fundamental physics from the real Universe, any claims of having measured a cosmological constant from directionally skewed, sparse samples of intrinsically scattered observations should have been taken with a grain of salt. By honouring this claim with a Nobel Prize, the Swedish Academy may have induced runaway prestige bias in favour of some of the least principled analyses in science, strengthening the confirmation bias that seems prevalent in cosmology.
          %p
            A conclusion along these lines, albeit weaker, is also reached independently in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#LinMackHou19"} Lin-Mack-Hou 19, p. 5
          %blockquote
            %p
              some proposals suggest some local/environmental factors (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 0.03
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \leq 0.03
              ) can bias the local determinations. This would mean the locally measured
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi H
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
              cannot be interpreted as the global Hubble constant of the homogeneous universe. An example is a local underdense region (Lombriser 2019; Shanks et al. 2019). Recent studies have shown observational evidence supporting a small-scale local underdense region (Boehringer et al. 2019; Pustilnik et al. 2019), though it has been argued that the likelihood for a local void to substantially affect the local measurement may be low (Kenworthyet al. 2019). These local factors do not pose a problem tothe standard ΛCDM model at large scales, but instead point to the need for a more detailed description of our local environment to account for such a systematic effect that can shift all local measurements in the same way. If all local measurements produce high values of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H_0
          %p
            A strong claim is due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#HeinesenBuchert20"} Heinesen-Buchert 20
          %blockquote
            %p a natural and consistent explanation of
            %p (i) dark energy,
            %p (ii) the coincidence problem (here conceptually,not quantitatively),
            %p (iii) positive initial curvature,
            %p (iv) the small matter density cosmological parameterfound in local probes of the matter density,
            %p (v) the large angular diameter distance tothe CMB consistent with JLA supernova sample parameter constraints,
            %p and (vi) the local expansion rate measurements (removal of the ‘Hubble tension’).
            %p We believe that this model architecture needs convincing arguments to be rejected as a physically viable show-case, on the basis of which the model ingredients can be improved in order to build a physical cosmology in the future.
          %p
            %a{:href => "#SLGRW25"}> SLGRW25
            \:
          %blockquote
            %p
              When considering the entire
              %code Pantheon+
              sample, we find very strong evidence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ln
                    %mi B
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mn 5
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (ln B \gt 5)
              in favour of timescape over
              = succeed "CDM." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda
          %h3#Impact Impact
          %p
            If the apparent small positive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"}> dark energy
            ) were but an artefact of neglecting backreaction of inhomogeneities, some theoretical puzzlements regarding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Sitter+spacetimes"} de Sitter spacetimes
            would disappear (see
            %a{:href => "de+Sitter+spacetime#Rajaraman16"} Rajaraman 16
            for general discussion and
            %a{:href => "#DanielssonVanRiet18"} Danielsson-VanRiet 18, p. 27
            for discussion in terms of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+string+theory+vacua"} perturbative string theory vacua
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten%27s+Dark+Fantasy"} Witten's Dark Fantasy
          %p
            In this context, notice that even a negative cosmological constant has been argued to still be compatible with observation if suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+fields"} scalar fields
            are present (
            %a{:href => "anti+de+Sitter+spacetime#SenAdilSen21"}> Sen, Adil &amp; Sen 2021
            )
          %h2#NumericalSimulation Numerical simulation
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+experiment"} Numerical simulations
            of inhomogeneous cosmology in the required
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relativistic+field+theory"} relativistic
            accuracy is in its infancy (see
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#BelejkoKorzynski16"} Belejko-Korzyński 16, p. 7
          %p
            %a{:href => "#ADDK13"}> ADDK 13
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ClesseRoisinFufza17"} Clesse-Roisin-Füzfa 17
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ACDDK17"} ACDDK 17
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MontanariRasanen17"} Montanari-Räsänen 17
            %a{:href => "#Adamek18"} Adamek 18
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gevolution"}> gevolution
            ”).
          %p
            The conclusion in
            %a{:href => "#MontanariRasanen17"} Montanari-Räsänen 17, p. 20
            is as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              the model
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              shows an increase of the expansion rate of the right order of magnitude, compared to observations, at late times.
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo .
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
          %p
            Dependency of results on the choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fixing"} gauge fixing
            is highlighted in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ACDDK17"} ACDDK 17
          %blockquote
            %p
              We then show numerical results from the fully relativistic weak field
              = succeed "-body" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              = succeed "." do
                %em gevolution
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              The conclusion of this work is therefore that there are gauges which are relatively close to what observers measure and in these gauges backreaction is small. We used the example of Poisson gauge, but there would be others, e.g. geodesic light cone gauge [53, 54]. However, comoving synchronous gauge is not well suited to describe observations in the late time clumpy universe. In this gauge backreaction becomes large and the gauge actually breaks down during structure formation. (p. 4)
          %p
            The simulations in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#OdderskovKoksbangHannestad16"} Odderskov-Koksbang-Hannestad 16
            %a{:href => "#MacphersonLaskyPrice18"} Macpherson-Lasky-Price 18
            show noticeable but small effects of inhomogeneity, possibly explaining parts but not all of the measured discrepancy reported in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#RiessEtAl16"} Riess et. al. 16
          %p
            Similarly
            %a{:href => "#Adamek18"} Adamek 18, slide 14
            on simulations obtained with
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gevolution"} gevolution
          %blockquote
            %p Backreaction is a real phenomenon that…
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  can be quantified accurately with
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computer+experiment"} numerical experiments
              %li
                %p
                  quantitatively cannot explain observed data without
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
              %li
                %p may nevertheless be relevant for precision cosmology with future surveys
          %p
            For more see also the pointers in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen18"} Räsänen 18, slide 7
          %h2#BackreactionDebate The “backreaction debate”
          %p
            A seminal theoretical argument that it
            %em is
            consistent to neglect cosmic inhomogeneity was given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GreenWald10"} Green-Wald 10
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GreenWald11"} Green-Wald 11
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GreenWald13"} Green-Wald 13
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#GreenWald16"} Green-Wald 16
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BuchertEtAl15"} Buchert et al. 15
            %a{:href => "#GreenWald15"} Green-Wald 15
            is summarized in
            %a{:href => "#OstrowskiRoukema15"} Ostrowski-Roukema 15, p. 4
            as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p Green and Wald state that their formalism does not apply to situations when
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              the actual metric (e.g., at recent epochs) is far from FLRW; or
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
              one wishes to construct an effective metric (or other effective quantities) through some averaging procedure
            %p This, in principle, ends the debate about whether backreaction has been excluded as a dark energy candidate: the Green and Wald formalism does not apply to the main body of backreaction research; backreaction remains a viable dark energy candidate.
          %p
            Later,
            %a{:href => "#Ostrowski19"} Ostrowski 19
            summarizes this as follows:
          %blockquote
            %p Green and Wald formalism, being a special case of two-scale asymptotic homogenization is not applicable to gravitational systems with hierarchical structures
            %p any features of backreaction (including backreaction being trace-free) based on Green and Wald formalism are unjustified
          %p#OpenProblem
            %strong Open problem
          %p
            Accordingly, the review
            %a{:href => "#Coley18"} Coley 18, section 3.5
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+relativity"} general relativity
            again regards the issue as open:
          %blockquote
            %p An important open question in cosmology is whether averaging of inhomogeneities can lead to significant backreaction effects on very large scales. (p. 28)
          %p
            Similarly in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#HuangGaoXu19"} Huang-Gao-Xu 19, p. 2
          %blockquote
            %p
              The lack of solid proof
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              is a more serious concern.
          %p
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#CosmoBack18"} CosmoBack 18
          %blockquote
            %p Indeed, the actual amplitude of the backreaction effect, whether it requires a fully nonlinear general relativistic treatment or whether a perturbative approach is sufficient, the impact of the gauge choice, of coarse-graining, and of averaging procedures in defining the observables are still open problems.
          %p
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Koksbang19"} Koksbang 19, p. 3
          %blockquote
            %p Whichever is the case, a theoretical quantification of cosmic backreaction is necessary for getting the foundations of cosmology onto solid ground; the mathematics clearly shows that in principle backreaction terms affect the overall dynamics of the Universe.
          %p
            Indeed,
            %a{:href => "#SmollerTempleVogler14"} Smoller-Temple-Vogler 14
            claim an analytic solution which does exhibit inhomogeneity effects mimicking dark energy (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#AbstractSmollerTempleVogler14"} above
            %a{:href => "#SarkarEtAl18"} Colin-Mohayaee-Rameez-Sarkar 19
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SarkarFlowQuote"} above
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#NumericalSimulation"} above
          %p
            A new analytical approach is suggested by
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Ginat20"} Ginat 20
          %h2#LemaitreTolmanBondi Lemaitre-Tolman-Bondi models
          %p
            A particular class of exactly soluable simple examples of inhomogeneous cosmological models are Lemaitre-Tolman-Bondi models. If taken as quasi-realistic models in themselves, these require assuming that we inhabit a position close to a singled-out “center” of the universe, usually the center of an assumed cosmic “void”, of low matter density. See e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Moffat05"} Moffat 05
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Enkvist07"} Enkvist 07
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Moffat16"} Moffat 16
            %a{:href => "#CliftonFerreiraLand08"} Clifton-Ferreira-Land 08
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#KBC13"} KBC 13
          %blockquote
            %p In particular, “minimal void” scenarios (e.g., Alexander et al. 2009; Bolejko &amp; Sussman 2011) have shown that very simple models placing the observer near the center of an under-density that is ∼ 300 Mpc in radius and roughly half the density of its surroundings are sufficient to explain the acceleration observed via type Ia supernovae. While these models are simplistic, they make clear that an observer’s location with respect to large-scale structures could have profound implications for that observer’s measurement of cosmological observables.
          %p
            It has been argued (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Moffat16"} Moffat 16, p. 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+formation"} structure formation
            (the “coincidence problem” of cosmology), and the perceived fine-tuning of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmological+constant"} cosmological constant
            required in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+cosmology"} standard model of cosmology
          %p
            However, this may be over-interpreting the realism of these simple models. According to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Rasanen03"} Räsänen 03, p. 15
          %blockquote
            %p In order to evaluate the importance of backreaction in the real universe, we need statistical knowledge about complex configurations of dust, not exact information about simplified models.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+formation"} structure formation
            %li
              %p
